fund-java/18-spring-web-controllers-and-.explanation:
Two levels, each with the same cause-chain search:

```java
String own = match(error, controller.handlers());
if (own != null) return own;
var ordered = new ArrayList<>(advices);
ordered.sort(Comparator.comparingInt(a -> a.order() == null ? Integer.MAX_VALUE : a.order()));
for (var advice : ordered) {
    if (advice.basePackage() != null && !controller.packageName().startsWith(advice.basePackage())) continue;
    String found = match(error, advice.handlers());
    if (found != null) return found;
}
return null;
// match: for (Throwable t = error; t != null; t = t.getCause()) { closest(t.getClass(), handlers) ... }
```

The tests catch the plausible wrong version that collects every handler from every advice and picks the globally closest type: it answers `precise:404` where Spring answers `broad:500`, and that difference is the classic "my handler is never called" bug, fixed with `@Order` (or by narrowing the broad advice). A version that checks the cause before the exception's own type fails the cause test, where a catch-all for `Exception` must win over the wrapped cause's handler.

