Filter in Spring's order and convert last:

```java
var byPath = routes.stream().filter(r -> pathMatches(r.template(), req.path())).toList();
if (byPath.isEmpty()) return new Outcome(404, null);
var byMethod = byPath.stream().filter(r -> r.method().equals(req.method())).toList();
if (byMethod.isEmpty()) return new Outcome(405, String.join(", ", new TreeSet<>(byPath.stream().map(Route::method).toList())));
// consumes -> 415, fewest variables wins, Long.parseLong each variable -> 400, else 200
```

The tests catch the plausible wrong version that converts while matching (treating `abc` as "no route", the ASP.NET Core route-constraint behaviour): it answers 404 where Spring answers 400, and 404 instead of 405 in the boundary test. A version that checks the method only after picking a single route reports 404 for `DELETE /api/orders`, and one that picks the first matching route instead of the most specific sends `/api/orders/export` to the `{id}` handler and answers 400.
